>> title: Har Gow
>> category: Dumplings & Rolls
>> tags: shrimp, dumpling, chinese, steamed, wheat starch
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: har gao, ha gow, haa gaau, xia jiao, 蝦餃, shrimp dumpling, crystal shrimp dumpling
>> pairs-with: siu-mai
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr 30 min
>> slack: narrow — six minutes and the skin goes translucent; steamed past that it splits along the pleats and dumps the shrimp into the basket
>> step.1: stir the boiling water in, knead hot, rest 10 min
>> step.2: stir one way until tacky, chill 30 min
>> step.3: roll 24 rounds, pleat the near edge only
>> step.4: steam 6 min, until the skin goes translucent

Stir @boiling water{3/4%cup}(180 mL) into @wheat starch{1%cup}(120 g), @tapioca starch{1/4%cup}(30 g) and @fine salt{1/4%tsp} all at once, work @lard{1%Tbs}(or vegetable shortening) in while the dough is still too hot to be comfortable, and ~rest{10%min} under an upturned bowl. Boiling water is what gelatinises wheat starch — warm water gives a dough that cracks at the pleat and steams up opaque.

Stir @raw shrimp{12%oz}(340 g; peeled, half chopped and half left whole) with @pork fatback{1%oz}(30 g; finely diced), @bamboo shoots{1/4%cup}(35 g; finely diced), @cornstarch{2%tsp}, @sugar{1%tsp}, @kosher salt{1/2%tsp}, @toasted sesame oil{1%tsp}, @Shaoxing wine{1%tsp} and @white pepper{1/4%tsp} in one direction until it turns tacky, then ~chill{30%min}.

Roll @&(~2)dough{} into a rope, cut it into 24 pieces, and press each one out to a 3-in round under an oiled cleaver, then close @&(~1)filling{} inside, pleating the near edge and leaving the far one plain so the pleats stand up.

Steam @&(~1)dumplings{} well apart in a #bamboo steamer{} brushed with @peanut oil{1%tsp}, over water at a hard boil, for ~steam{6%min}.
